ES, PRODUCTIVITY AND THE ENVIRONMENT © OECD 2015 33\nL1183@P34: REFERENCES\nL1184@P34: • EEA (European Environment Agency) (2010), The European Environment: State and Outlook 2010 –Material\nL1185@P34: Resources and Waste, http://www.eea.europa.eu/soer/europe/material-resources-and-waste.\nL1186@P34: • European Commission, Resource Efficiency,\nL1529@P44: the environmental impacts associated with final consumption (UNEP, 2010b). In industrialised\nL1530@P44: countries, housing, mobility, food and manufactured products determine about 70% of the impacts\nL1531@P44: of household consumption, while in developing and emerging economies, consumption related to\nL1532@P44: food and housing is responsible for most of the impact, while mobility is less of a factor.\nL1533@P44: Consumption patterns are complex and changing consumer behaviour is challenging. The\nL1534@P44: consumption of one product is often linked to the consumption of other products, or determined by\nL1535@P44: history or national circumstance.\nL1536@P44: ENDNOTES\nL1537@P44: 1 According to the FAO (2010b) the figure is “undoubtedly higher” considering that informally and illegally\nL1538@P44: removed wood is not captured. Wood fuel is the most commonly under-reported category. Wood fuel accounts\nL1539@P44: for about half of the wood removed globally.\nL1540@P44: 2 Data based on OECD analysis of UN COMTRADE data. Trade is measured as one-way trade (i.e. exports). The\nL1541@P44: sample includes 178 countries. “Raw, semi-finished and finished products” are defined materials and products\nL1542@P44: falling under the following broad material categories: cereals, wood, paper, fish, meat, dairy, fossil fuels, ferrous\nL1543@P44: and non-ferrous metals and phosphates as well as products mainly derived from these materials. This list\nL1544@P44: corresponds largely with the material categories outlined in Eurostat’s questionnaire on economy-wide material\nL1545@P44: flow accounts, with the exception of non-metallic, non-energy minerals and some types of food biomass (e.g.\nL1546@P44: fruits, vegetables). Save for phosphate rock, non-metallic minerals are rarely traded between countries and their\nL1547@P44: inclusion/exclusion is not expected to materially influence trade flows (when expressed in terms of weight or\nL1548@P44: volume).\nL1549@P44:  (http://epp.eurostat.ec.europa.eu/portal/page/portal/environmental_accounts/methodology/data_collections )\nL1550@P44: 3 These findings are consistent with World Bank (2010), which found that in the decade prior to the global\nL1551@P44: economic and financial crisis, the value of global trade in natural resources had been growing 20% annually,\nL1552@P44: rising from USD 0.6 trillion in 1998 to 3.7 trillion in 2008 and increasing its share of total trade from 11% to 24%\nL2265@P59: further development of MF and resource productivity indicators, and the sharing of good practices\nL2266@P59: within countries, among countries and among enterprises.\nL2267@P59: Better information will in turn help make a strong case for policies aimed at improving resource\nL2268@P59: productivity by showing the full benefits of such policies, and will contribute to a better analysis of\nL2269@P59: progress towards green growth.\nL2270@P59: The OECD will continue to develop internationally harmonised material flow and resource\nL2271@P59: productivity data and indicators (both at macro level and for selected materials such as critical raw\nL2272@P59: materials and materials raising particular environmental concerns) and to promote the exchange of\nL2273@P59: related experience among countries. This is done in collaboration with UNEP and its International\nL2274@P59: Resource Panel, Eurostat and several research institutes. International cooperation has also been\nL2275@P59: established to advance the measurement and knowledge of green growth (GGKP, OECD, WB,\nL2276@P59: UNEP).\nL2277@P59: ENDNOTES\nL2278@P59: 1 The report Resource Productivity i