ber countries and BRIICS\nL2653@P68: countries, but does not include world totals. The material flows database by the Sustainable Europe Research\nL2654@P68: Institute (SERI) and the Vienna University (available at www.materialflows.net) has data on domestic\nL2655@P68: extraction used (DEU) and unused domestic extraction (UDE) for 188 countries from which world totals were\nL2656@P68: estimated. For consistency, OECD and BRIICS countries’ global shares were calculated based on SERI/WU\nL2657@P68: data rather than data from the OECD material flows dataset.\nL2658@P68: 2 Projection by Wuppertal Institute based on business as usual scenario.\nL2659@P68: 3 In the case of construction minerals, information sources have estimated missing data from non-missing\nL2660@P68: values and trends in GDP (e.g. in SERI, 2010). As a result, correlations between the supply or demand of\nL2661@P68: construction minerals and macroeconomic data as GDP must be interpreted with caution (Dittrich, 2010).\nL2662@P68: 4 SERI has estimated that global soil erosion from agricultural land ranges between 25-50 billion tonnes a year.\nL2663@P68: The indicator UDE in the OECD material flow database does not account for soil erosion. Estimating soil\nL2664@P68: erosion is complex and beyond the scope of this report.\nL2665@P68: 740\nL2666@P68: 848\nL2667@P68: 953\nL2668@P68: 438\nL2669@P68: 496\nL2670@P68: 584\nL2671@P68: 0\nL2672@P68: 200\nL2673@P68: 400\nL2674@P68: 600\nL2675@P68: 800\nL2676@P68: 1000\nL2677@P68: 1200\nL2678@P68: 1980 1990 2010\nL2679@P68: GDP per unit of DEU\nL2680@P68: GDP per unit of DEU+UDE\nL2681@P68: 1000 USD per tonne\nL2682@P68: 012345\nL2683@P68: 1980\nL2684@P68: 1990\nL2685@P68: 2010\nL2686@P68: tonne per person per year\nL2687@P68: Construction &\nL2688@P68: industrial\nL2689@P68: minerals\nL2690@P68: Metals\nL2691@P68: Fossil\nL2692@P68: energy\nL2693@P68: carriers\nL2694@P68-69: BiomassCHAPTER 4. THE MATERIAL BASIS OF THE GLOBAL ECONOMY\nL4658@P103: increase significantly in coming decades as stocks are discarded and replaced.\nL4659@P103: Tracking anthropogenic stocks can be used as a planning tool, not only for estimating the future\nL4660@P103: availability of scrap, but also for understanding consumption patterns and barriers to recovery and\nL4661@P103: recycling. As such, increasing our knowledge about anthropogenic stocks is an integral to\nL4662@P103: improving resource productivity. Anthropogenic stock estimation is a growing area of study. But\nL4663@P103: much work still needs to be done, particularly to close gaps with regards to less developed countries\nL4664@P103: and specialty metals.\nL4665@P103: ENDNOTES\nL4666@P103: 1 Figures do not include Slovenia or Estonia as historical data on the material flows of these countries prior to\nL4667@P103: the early 1990s is limited.\nL4668@P103: 2 Precious metals include gold, silver and the six platinum group metals (iridium, osmium, palladium,\nL4669@P103: platinum, rhodium, and ruthenium).\nL4670@P103: 3\nL4671@P103:  Jackson, T. (2009). Prosperity without Growth: The Transition to a Sustainable Economy. United Kingdom:\nL4672@P103: Sustainable Development Commission. 48-49.\nL4673@P103: 4 Several studies have suggested that the intensity of use of a mineral (the use of a mineral commodity divided\nL4674@P103: by GDP) depends on the level of economic development as measured by GDP per capita, and that the pattern\nL4675@P103: of intensity of use follows an inverse U-shape as economies develop). As development takes place, countries\nL4676@P103: focus on building infrastructure (such as rails, roads, and bridges, housing and other buildings and water\nL4677@P103: supply and electricity transmission) and people buy more durable goods, which rapidly increases the demand\nL4678@P103: for mineral commodities. As economies mature, all other things being equal, they move to a less materials\u0002intensive phase, spending more on education and other services, which reduces the 