t side (e.g. SO2 emissions).\nL5936@P129: ENDNOTES\nL5937@P129: 1 The U.S. Geological Survey defines the term resource as “A concentration of naturally occurring solid, liquid,\nL5938@P129: or gaseous material in or on the Earth’s crust in such form and amount that economic extraction of a\nL5939@P129: commodity from the concentration is currently or potentially feasible.” This is much broader than the reserve\nL5940@P129: base, which is defined as “that part of an identified resource that meets specified minimum physical and\nL5941@P129: chemical criteria related to current mining and production practices, including those for grade, quality,\nL5942@P129: thickness, and depth.” It includes those resources that are currently economic (reserves), marginally\nL5943@P129: economic (marginal reserves), and some of those that are currently sub-economic (sub-economic resources).\nL5944@P129: The U.S. Geological Survey stopped publishing figures for reserve base after 2009 due to difficulties in\nL5945@P129: estimating the non-reserve component. Reserve and resource estimates continue to be published.\nL6410@P138: will be some time before discarded flows are large enough to contribute meaningfully to steel\nL6411@P138: production. However, evidence that per capita iron stocks reach a saturation point in post\u0002industrial economies offers the long-term possibility of transforming the global iron and steel\nL6412@P138: industry from one that relies predominantly on primary (iron ore) sources to one that relies\nL6413@P138: predominantly on secondary (ferrous scrap) sources, assuming the world’s population also\nL6414@P138-139: stabilises. IRON AND STEEL FACTSHEET\nL6415@P139: 138 MATERIAL RESOURCES, PRODUCTIVITY AND THE ENVIRONMENT © OECD 2015\nL6416@P139: Figure 5. World map of per capita iron ore reserves versus per capita in use iron stocks, 2005\nL6417@P139: Note: Country sizes are distorted in proportion to the size of their absolute reserves or in use stocks. Colour 