6953@P149: REFERENCES\nL6954@P149: • BIR (Bureau of International Recycling), BIR website, www.bir.org\nL7308@P156: prepared food products that contain a greater proportion of animal protein. Meat- and dairy\u0002based diets are more phosphate intensive and it is estimated that changing from the average\nL7309@P156: western diet to a vegetarian diet could decrease phosphorus fertilizers demand by at least 20-\nL7310@P156: 45% (Cordell, Drangert and White, 2009). Global per capita meat consumption is expected to\nL7311@P156: increase by 9% (or 60 Mt), led by consumption growth in Asia (OECD-FAO, 2011). Although\nL7312@P156: per capita consumption levels are largely stable in OECD countries, they are relatively high\nL7313@P156: and are expected to remain well above levels in non-OECD countries for the foreseeable\nL7314@P156: future.5\nL7315@P156: ENDNOTES\nL7316@P156: 1 The P2O5 content of phosphate normally ranges between 25 and 40% and is on average close to 30-35%\nL7317@P156: (USGS, 2001).\nL7318@P156: 2 The largest amount of phosphorus, 27 to 840 peta (1015) tonnes, is stored in oceans (80 to 120 Gt in sea water\nL7319@P156: and the remainder in the sea bed in the form of insoluble calcium phosphate) (Liu et al., 2008).\nL7320@P156: 3 In 2010, the U.S. Geological Survey reported global phosphate rock reserves of 16 Gt. Figures for Morocco\nL7321@P156: were revised in 2011 using new information from the Moroccan producer and a report by the International\nL7322@P156: Fertilizer Development Centre.\nL7323@P156: 4 The Western Sahara is a disputed territory currently administered by Morocco.\nL7324@P156: 5 For example, in 2020 per capita beef consumption in non-OECD countries is expected to be 5.0 kg/person\nL7325@P156-157: compared to 14.9 kg/person on average in OECD countries (OECD-FAO, 2011). PHOSPHORUS FACTSHEET\nL7326@P157: 156 MATERIAL RESOURCES, PRODUCTIVITY AND THE ENVIRONMENT © OECD 2015\nL7327@P157: REFERENCES\nL7328@P157: • BGS (British Geological Survey) (2010), World mineral production 2004-08, British Geological Survey, Keyworth,\nL7329@P157: Nottingham.\nL7688@P165: important to ensure that the potential environmental impacts of REE extraction and processing are\nL7689@P165: not overlooked in the race to meet growing demand. As with other metals, air pollution, water\nL7690@P165: contamination, habitat destruction and carbon-based gas emissions can result from REE\nL7691@P165: production. But for rare earths, the refinement process is made all the more complex (and risky\nL7692@P165: from an environmental impacts point of view) due to their chemical and radioactive properties. A\nL7693@P165: detailed material flow analysis of rare earths would help to better understand the environmental\nL7694@P165: implications of their different end uses.\nL7695@P165: Endnotes\nL7696@P165: 1 The abundance of individual REE depends on the element’s atomic number (even numbers are more\nL7697@P165: abundant) and whether the element is light or heavy (light REE are more abundant).\nL7698@P165: 2 REE figures refer to rare earth oxide (REO) equivalents. The REE-to-REO ratio for each of the REEs is\nL7699@P165: around 1:0.85. The relative fractions of REOs by end-use closely approximate the relative REE end uses.\nL7700@P165: 3 See Schüler et al., (2011) for a detailed list of research activities on recycling REE by end-use.\nL7701@P165: 4 “Reserves” are defined by USGS as the estimated amount of the material in such a form or amount of\nL7702@P165: concentration that economic extraction is currently or potentially feasible.\nL7703@P165: 5 Rare earth elements are rarely traded as ores or concentrates. In 2009 rare earth compounds accounted for\nL7704@P165: nearly 90% of total world exports. REE metals and alloys made up 10% of exports.\nL7705@P165: 6 The four bills are: The Rare Earths and Critical Materials Revitalization Act of 2011 (H.R. 618), the RARE Act\nL7706@P165: of 2011 (H.R. 1314), the Rare Earths Supply Chain Technology and Resources Transformation Act of 2011\nL7707@P165: (H.R. 1388) and the Rare Earth Policy Task Force and Materials Act (H.R. 2184).\nL7708@P165: 7 Toyota and Honda have established recycling programmes, offering customers 200 USD for each battery\n"
